<fragment>
  <!-- Shared by every project model API (models/<project>), inbound, after model-api-route. Deployed by
       gateway-policy-deployment. Every prompt sent to a platform model (agent calls and evaluation judges) is checked
       by Azure AI Content Safety (backend "content-safety", APIM managed identity) before it reaches the model:
         - harm categories Hate, Sexual, SelfHarm, Violence, blocked at severity 4 or above (scale 0-7)
         - Prompt Shields (shield-prompt): blocks jailbreak and prompt-injection attempts
       Per project and model: the project's model API policy (in the requested model's routing-table branch) sets
       cs-hate, cs-sexual, cs-self-harm, cs-violence (int, 0-7), cs-prompt-shields and cs-check-answers (bool) from project.yaml: the
       model's models.allowed[].content_safety, else the project's models.content_safety. A setting neither passes
       uses the platform default here: severity 4 for each category, Prompt Shields on. Settings can only be stricter
       (validated by agent-ci and the model-project-connection plan).
       One platform exception: a judge model (model.yaml purpose: judge, in the registry) gets cs-prompt-shields false
       from onboarding. A judge prompt is the evaluator's instructions around a model's answer, written by the platform;
       Prompt Shields reads it as an injection attempt and blocked some judge calls (403). Harm categories still apply.
       The check scores what the USER sent: the latest user message. The rest of the request (Foundry's developer
       message "You are an AI assistant accessed via an API...", the agent's instructions, history, tool results) is
       written by the platform and the agent; llm-content-safety scores all text it gets as one, and with that framing
       a harmful message would score far lower than on its own. So the fragment saves the request, gives the policy a
       copy with only the last user message, and restores the full request for the model. Earlier user messages were
       checked when they were sent.
       ANSWERS are checked too only when the project opts in (cs-check-answers true, from project.yaml
       content_safety.check_answers; platform default OFF), with the same categories and thresholds: a flagged answer
       is replaced by 403; a streamed answer (Foundry streams its model calls) is checked in 1,000-character windows
       and forwarding stops at the first flagged window (no 403 then: the agent gets a cut-off answer). Each checked
       answer costs one Content Safety call per 1,000 characters and adds latency.
       A blocked prompt gets 403 and never reaches the model, so it uses no tokens. Runs after the kill switches and the
       allowed-model check, so suspended or refused calls cost no Content Safety call. The model deployment's own
       guardrail (model.yaml guardrail) still applies too, including to the model's answers. -->
  <set-variable name="cs-original-body" value="@(context.Request.Body.As&lt;string&gt;(true))" />
  <set-body>@{
    var request = JObject.Parse((string)context.Variables["cs-original-body"]);
    var messages = request["messages"] as JArray ?? new JArray();
    var lastUser = messages.LastOrDefault(m => (string)m["role"] == "user");
    var check = new JObject();
    check["messages"] = lastUser == null ? new JArray() : new JArray(lastUser.DeepClone());
    return check.ToString();
  }</set-body>
  <llm-content-safety backend-id="content-safety" enforce-on-completions="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;bool&gt;(&quot;cs-check-answers&quot;, false))" shield-prompt="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;bool&gt;(&quot;cs-prompt-shields&quot;, true))">
    <categories output-type="EightSeverityLevels">
      <category name="Hate" threshold="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;int&gt;(&quot;cs-hate&quot;, 4))" />
      <category name="Sexual" threshold="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;int&gt;(&quot;cs-sexual&quot;, 4))" />
      <category name="SelfHarm" threshold="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;int&gt;(&quot;cs-self-harm&quot;, 4))" />
      <category name="Violence" threshold="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;int&gt;(&quot;cs-violence&quot;, 4))" />
    </categories>
  </llm-content-safety>
  <!-- The model gets the full request (instructions, history, tools), not the cut-down copy that was checked. -->
  <set-body>@((string)context.Variables["cs-original-body"])</set-body>
</fragment>
